Name: move file to trash

Type: command

Syntax: move file <pathname> to trash

Summary:
Moves a <file> to the user's Trash or Recycle Bin, from which it can be
put back.

Introduced: 9.7.1

OS: mac, windows, linux

Platforms: desktop

Security: disk

Example:
move file "/Users/me/Desktop/old-notes.txt" to trash

Example:
move file tFilePath to trash
if the result is not empty then
   answer "Could not move the file to the trash:" && the result
end if

Parameters:
pathname:
Specifies the name and location of the file. If you specify a name but
not a location, the engine looks for the file in the <defaultFolder>.

The result:
Empty when the file went to the trash. If there is no file or folder at
<pathname>, the result is "can't find that file". If it cannot go to
the trash for any other reason (on a phone or a server, where there is
no trash, or when the system refuses), the result is "can't move to
trash" and the file is where it was.

Description:
Use the <move file to trash> <command> to remove a file the way the
desktop's own file manager does. Unlike <delete file>, which removes the
file for good, it moves the file to the Trash (on macOS and Linux) or
the Recycle Bin (on Windows), from which the user can put it back or
empty it.

If the file is open (with <open file>), it is closed first, as
<delete file> does.

A <symbolic link> (one made with <create alias> on Linux or macOS) goes
to the trash itself: the file or folder it points to stays where it is.

On macOS the system's own trash takes the file, which handles files on
other volumes too. On Windows the shell sends it to the Recycle Bin. On
Linux, `gio trash` takes it when it is there (every desktop with a trash
has it); otherwise the file goes to the user's trash as the
freedesktop.org Trash specification lays it out, with a note of where it
came from, when it is on the same file system.

>*Note:* "trash" is no word of the language: a variable, a control or
> a custom property called trash means what it always did, and
> <move file to trash> is read only in this form, after the word file
> (or folder). This command is new in OXT-Beyond 0.2.8-dp.3, from
> HyperXTalk, where Emily-Elizabeth Howard wrote it; earlier versions
> did not compile it.

References: move folder to trash (command), delete file (command),
delete folder (command), show in finder (command),
defaultFolder (property), open file (command), file (keyword),
create alias (command), symbolic link (glossary), command (glossary)

Tags: file system
